<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Odysseas Androutsos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1788">1788</a>–<a href="/years/1825">1825</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Odysseas Androutsos was a Greek military leader of the War of Independence, active in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-greece">Central Greece</a>. He won fame by holding off a much larger Ottoman force at the inn of Gravia in 1821, but his independent conduct brought him into conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/areopagus">Areopagus</a> and the provisional government. Accused of treason, he was imprisoned on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acropolis-of-athens">Acropolis of Athens</a> and killed there in 1825.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/odysseas-androutsos.jpg" alt="Odysseas Androutsos" width="320" height="478" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adam Friedel · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Odysseas_Androutsos_by_Adam_Friedel_1830.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Born Odysseas Verousis in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ithaca">Ithaca</a>, he was the son of Andreas Verousis, an Albanian klepht from Livanates who sailed as a privateer with Lambros Katsonis, and of Akrivi Tsarlampa of Preveza. His father was captured in 1792 and died in an Ottoman jail, and Odysseas grew up with his mother's family of notables in the Ionian Islands. Between 1805 and 1810 he joined the army of Ali Pasha at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannina">Ioannina</a>, by March 1814 led his personal guard, learned Albanian and Italian, and joined the Bektashi order.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1816 Ali Pasha made him armatolos of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livadeia">Livadeia</a>, where he restored order, curbed the local notables by despotic methods and became the strongest man of arms in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-greece">Central Greece</a>. He joined the Filiki Eteria in 1818 or 1820. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> moved against Ali in 1820 his own men submitted, and he lost Livadeia to his lieutenant Athanasios Diakos. At Gravia in May 1821 he held an inn with about 100 men against Omer Vrioni, who lost over 300 dead, and slipped away before reinforcements came, losing six men.</p><p>Made commander of eastern mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, he quarrelled with the government. When it sent Christos Palaskas and Alexios Noutsos to replace him in 1822 he had both killed, and Ioannis Kolettis and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/areopagus">Areopagus</a> accused him of collaboration. In September 1822 he took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acropolis-of-athens">Acropolis of Athens</a> with Yannis Gouras and Yannis Makriyannis. In late 1822 he offered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> to recognise their authority in return for a hereditary armatolik, later calling such dealings a ruse. In 1825 he received an Ottoman amnesty on 31 March and fought beside Ottoman troops before Greek insurgents captured him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Held without trial in the Frankish Tower of the Acropolis, he was tortured and executed on 5 June 1825 on the order of his former lieutenant Gouras, and his body was thrown from the rock. It was reburied in 1865, and in 1967 his bones were moved to Preveza. Thomas Gordon called him bloodthirsty and treacherous; Finlay said he aped Ali's tyranny. Others, such as Vasileios Sfyroeras, argue that chieftains' negotiations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> often served the Greek cause, and Greek tradition holds him a tragic hero.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Odysseas Androutsos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It struggled to control powerful armed chieftains, above all <b>Odysseas Androutsos</b>, with whom it was in open conflict.</blockquote><a href="/state/areopagus-of-eastern-continental-greece">Areopagus of Eastern Continental Greece</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Frankish Tower, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.97,23.73&amp;year=1812" aria-label="Frankish Tower on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Odysseas Androutsos"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4578 4309h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4578 4309h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=37.97,23.73&amp;year=1812">What was here in 1812? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
